WEBVTT

00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:19.137
<v Jamie>The Army Secretary just walked out mid-Pentagon civil war, Tesla's up five point five percent while the rest of the market's basically napping, and I'm wondering if we're living in two separate economies or just one really exhausting one.
  ↳ source: https://seekingalpha.com/news/4638522-u-s-army-secretary-driscoll-resigns-after-pentagon-leadership-rift?utm_source=feed_news_all&utm_medium=referral&feed_item_type=news

00:00:19.137 --> 00:00:44.955
<v Alex>So yesterday mortgage rates spiked to the highest since June twenty twenty-five, and we're all acting shocked—but the real story is that geopolitical events are now pricing into housing faster than the Fed's own guidance. A Middle East flare-up moves oil, oil moves energy costs, energy costs move inflation expectations, and suddenly your thirty-year fixed jumps half a percent overnight. The Fed's lost the sole control dial. They're one input among many, and that's actually a bigger structural shift than the rate number itself.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/31/mortgage-rates-surge-middle-east-attacks-oil.html

00:00:44.955 --> 00:01:05.305
<v Alex>So the S&P is down zero point three three percent, Nasdaq basically flat at minus zero point one two, but Tesla's up five point five one percent—which means one stock is doing more work than the entire rest of the market combined. Meanwhile, the VIX jumped three point four percent, and the ten-year Treasury spiked. This is what breadth collapse looks like: the headline number lies.

00:01:05.305 --> 00:01:11.380
<v Jamie>Wait, so oil's moving the Treasury faster than the Fed's own statements now, and we're just... watching it happen?

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:34.767
<v Alex>Welcome to having no control dial. Amazon's down two point five percent, Google down two point zero nine, Microsoft down one point two two—all the names that were supposed to carry us through a soft landing are bleeding out. Energy stocks are the only thing propping up the scoreboard. Exxon's up two point seven one, Chevron up two point twelve. The market's basically a patient on life support being kept alive by a petroleum IV drip.

00:01:34.767 --> 00:01:46.309
<v Maya>So tech's getting crushed because rates are spiking on geopolitical oil panic, and the only winners are the ones that benefit from that exact same panic—it's like the market's rewarding the arsonist and punishing the fire department.

00:01:46.309 --> 00:02:00.281
<v Alex>Nvidia's only up one point four eight percent. Lilly's down one point five two. When the mega-cap growth names that own the decade are limping, and oil majors are the only thing standing—that's not a correction, that's a regime shift pretending to be a Tuesday.

00:02:00.281 --> 00:02:09.697
<v Jamie>So when your Defense Secretary is actively undermining your Army Secretary's diplomatic channel, you don't have a policy disagreement. You have a chain of command that's broken in real time.
  ↳ source: https://seekingalpha.com/news/4638522-u-s-army-secretary-driscoll-resigns-after-pentagon-leadership-rift?utm_source=feed_news_all&utm_medium=referral&feed_item_type=news

00:02:09.697 --> 00:02:18.201
<v Alex>The market didn't flinch. Defense stocks are flat. That's the real signal—investors aren't pricing in instability at the Pentagon. They're pricing in that this is normal now.

00:02:18.201 --> 00:02:34.603
<v Maya>The part that actually scares me: without that bridge between the military institution and whoever's running foreign policy, there's nobody translating anymore. When the next crisis hits—and it will—the Army and the White House are going to be operating off completely different maps, and nobody will even know it until it's too late.

00:02:34.603 --> 00:02:40.981
<v Jamie>And Tesla's still up five and a half percent because the market's decided that's not a problem to price in.

00:02:40.981 --> 00:02:49.486
<v Alex>Trump says U.S. growth could hit twenty percent. It's happened once since World War Two—nineteen fifty-one, during Korean War mobilization. That's it. One time. In seventy-three years.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/31/trump-us-gdp-growth-20-percent-economy.html

00:02:49.486 --> 00:02:57.383
<v Jamie>Wait, so he's comparing peacetime policy to a literal war economy and acting like it's a math problem you solve with the right tax cut?

00:02:57.383 --> 00:03:09.532
<v Alex>The argument is growth that fast shouldn't prompt rate hikes, even though inflation's still above two percent. Which is saying the Fed should ignore its mandate because the numerator got really big. That's not policy; that's a hostage note.

00:03:09.532 --> 00:03:22.896
<v Maya>The FTC just sued Amazon for running a hidden ad surcharge scheme—and I mean hidden as in the company literally engineered it to be invisible. They're accusing the pioneer of hidden fees of hiding fees on the infrastructure they built to hide fees.

00:03:22.896 --> 00:03:30.490
<v Jamie>So Amazon invented the whole 'bury the real cost' playbook and now they're getting sued for playing their own game better than everyone else?

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:45.069
<v Maya>Multiple state attorneys general plus the FTC. The allegation is they systematically overcharged advertisers on the platform, and Amazon kept the spread. Which, yes, is exactly what an ad network does—but they're saying Amazon did it in a way that violated antitrust law by leveraging their marketplace dominance.

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:53.574
<v Alex>Mortgage rates just hit their highest level since June twenty twenty-five. Middle East oil spike pushed them there. The housing market is now a hostage to geopolitics.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/31/mortgage-rates-surge-middle-east-attacks-oil.html

00:03:53.574 --> 00:04:02.078
<v Jamie>But we spent the whole first half talking about how the Fed's lost control of the dial. This is just that thesis playing out in real estate.

00:04:02.078 --> 00:04:12.405
<v Alex>Right. The expectation was falling rates this year. Instead, a war that has nothing to do with the U.S. housing market just made borrowing more expensive. Nobody planned for that in Q-one earnings.

00:04:12.405 --> 00:04:20.302
<v Maya>Retailers are handling tariff refunds like a choose-your-own-adventure book—some are passing savings to consumers, some are pocketing the margin. Same event, completely different earnings stories.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/30/trump-tariff-refunds-walmart-home-depot-target.html

00:04:20.302 --> 00:04:23.947
<v Jamie>So the consumer gets whatever ending the CFO decided was most convenient?

00:04:23.947 --> 00:04:30.022
<v Maya>Exactly. Which means tariff relief isn't a policy—it's a quarterly earnings narrative each company gets to write for itself.

00:04:30.022 --> 00:04:37.008
<v Maya>Tesla's five point five percent gain is what happens when one asset class stops asking permission from the rest of the market.

00:04:37.008 --> 00:04:40.045
<v Alex>It's not hedging anymore—it's just indifference to the room.

00:04:40.045 --> 00:04:44.297
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
